1. blocking — brief:90 `opusWorktree` collapses a non-zero `git worktree list` into no entry, while `lstatSafe` collapses every filesystem error into absence. A plausible damaged `.git/worktrees` entry or mistakenly non-searchable `.worktrees/` therefore makes the real `next --perform --expect cleanup` caller remove or skip a possibly registered folder and delete its branches; no existing row catches either failure. Replace brief:90 with: `| 1. The git worktree listing and <repo>/.worktrees/<id> | listWorktrees succeeds with a valid listing; the candidate is absent, or is a real direct child with the stated contents | cleanup (extended) holds before mutation when listWorktrees returns undefined or candidate lstat fails; clearLeftover rejects invalid, unreadable, symlinked or non-empty candidates |`. [severity: S1] [design: keeps] [class: spec×input-domain-missing-or-unenforceable] — check: packages/cli/src/next.test.ts W-227-b1
2. blocking — brief:94 `commandSeq` does not expose command substitutions inside double quotes, so an ordinary main-thread call such as `echo "$(cd .worktrees/W-1 && pwd)"` executes the `cd` but `worktreeCd` allows it; W-227-b4 tests only forms the parser already exposes. Replace brief:94’s `in ( ) or $( )` with `in ( ) or $( ), including a command substitution inside double quotes`, and pin that quoted form in W-227-b4. [severity: S1] [design: keeps] [class: spec×promise-unattainable-under-accepted-domain] — check: packages/cli/src/guard.test.ts W-227-b4
